  <h1>Four Days on the Garden Isle</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Kauaʻi's North Shore, a valley of taro, and a canyon at sunset.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>April 22–26, 2026 · four days of photos</strong><br />
    Līhuʻe → lunch on Kalapaki Bay → north on the Kūhiō Highway → Princeville → Hanalei → Hanalei Valley Lookout →
    Kīlauea → Kapaʻa → Poʻipū → Waimea Canyon Drive → Red Dirt Falls<br />
    One rental car · one nēnē · one cat in an egg chair · a beach of green sea turtles ·
    one canyon in the last hour of sun
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-kauai.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-kauai.jpg'} alt="Map of Kauaʻi showing the stops in order: Līhuʻe and Kalapaki Bay on the east side, north along the Kūhiō Highway past Anahola to Princeville, the Hanalei Valley Lookout, Kīlauea and Hanalei on the North Shore, then south to Poʻipū and west to Waimea Canyon and Red Dirt Falls." /></a>
    <figcaption>Four days around Kauaʻi, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    In April 2026 we spent April 22 to 26 on Kauaʻi. We stayed on the North
    Shore, in Princeville, and used a rental car to get everywhere else. The camera rolls cover the first four days.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything below is put together from the camera roll: what was photographed,
    where, and at what minute. This time most of that camera roll is my wife's. I took only a few dozen photos on
    this trip; she took a few hundred. My own phone only woke up properly at the end, at
    Waimea Canyon, where we were both photographing the same view. All times are Hawaiʻi time.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Līhuʻe and Kalapaki Bay</h2>
  <p>
    My wife's first photo of the trip is the wing of our plane on the runway at <strong>Līhuʻe</strong>, at 11:37 on
    Wednesday morning, with the ocean just beyond the end of the airfield. A quarter of an hour later we were on the shuttle to
    the rental lot, and by a quarter past twelve we were driving out of the airport.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wing-lihue.jpg'} alt="A white airplane wing over the runway, with green grass and a strip of blue ocean beyond the airfield under a light blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On the ground in Līhuʻe, 11:37 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lunch was a few minutes away at <strong>Duke's Kauaʻi</strong>, on <strong>Kalapaki Bay</strong> in Nāwiliwili. The
    restaurant opens onto a lawn with palms and a paved path, and beyond the lawn is the beach and the bay, with a
    green ridge rising on the far side of the water. Next to the path stood a small green stall hung with aloha-print
    dresses, and in front of it, a rooster was walking across the grass.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalapaki-bay.jpg'} alt="A lawn with tall palms and a tiki torch post above a sandy beach, with a blue umbrella, catamarans and a steep green ridge across the bay." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Kalapaki Bay from the lawn at Duke's, 12:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rooster.jpg'} alt="A red and black rooster walking on grass in front of a green stall hung with four aloha-print dresses." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A rooster outside the dress stall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We ate on the open-sided terrace, looking out at the bay, and finished with a slice of chocolate pie. After lunch
    we stayed in Līhuʻe a little longer for a <strong>cat café</strong>. Most of my wife's photos there are of other
    visitors with the cats. The one without anyone in it shows a tabby curled up in the middle of a rattan egg chair,
    among the cushions.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cat-chair.jpg'} alt="A tabby cat curled up on white cushions inside a round rattan egg chair, against a bamboo-paneled wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cat café, 2:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'purple-cake.jpg'} alt="A small round mousse cake with a deep purple top and a cream layer below, topped with a star of whipped cream and a purple jelly cube." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A purple mousse cake, 2:55 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then came a small purple mousse cake, with a cream layer underneath and a star of whipped cream on top: the first
    of two purple things on this trip.
  </p>

  <h2>North to Princeville</h2>
  <p>
    In the late afternoon we drove north on the <strong>Kūhiō Highway</strong>, the road up the east side of the island.
    At 4:38, near Anahola, I took one of my few photos of the first day: the road running under a dark canopy of trees,
    straight toward a jagged peak, with the sun breaking through the clouds above it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'anahola-road.jpg'} alt="A two-lane road running under trees toward a jagged green peak, with the sun flaring through clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kūhiō Highway near Anahola, 4:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Twenty minutes later we turned into <strong>Princeville</strong>, the resort town on the bluffs at the north end of the
    island. Its entrance is a roundabout with a fountain, and behind it stand rows of tall, narrow pines, each one shaped
    like a column. We had booked a rental house in Princeville for the whole stay, and after checking in we drove down the
    hill to <strong>Hanalei</strong>, the small town on Hanalei Bay, where the gate of Ching Young Village was lit up with
    red lanterns at dusk.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-pines.jpg'} alt="A fountain in a grassy roundabout with rows of tall columnar pines behind it, seen through a car windshield." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The way into Princeville, 4:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ching-young.jpg'} alt="A green wooden gate with a red Ching Young Village sign, red lanterns and small shops along a walkway at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ching Young Village, Hanalei, 7:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    When we got back that evening, a roach crawled out of the bed. We decided not to stay
    there at all, moved out that same night, and booked the Westin Princeville instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 2: A morning at the Westin Princeville</h2>
  <p>
    So Thursday morning began at the <strong>Westin Princeville Ocean Resort Villas</strong>, which also
    has an entry in my <a href="/misc/hotels">hotels post</a>, and we spent it at the pools. The main pool is built like a lagoon, with boulders at the
    edges and thatched shelters around it, and at 8:21 in the morning the water was still enough to hold a perfect
    reflection of the palms and the clouds.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'westin-pool.jpg'} alt="A still lagoon-style pool reflecting palm trees and white clouds, with thatched shelters and loungers on the far side under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lagoon pool, 8:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few steps away, a stream runs down through black rock channels from a row of small waterfalls, and it is full
    of koi: orange, white, and orange-and-white, crowding toward the inflow. On the ocean side, another pool looks out
    over hedges and palms to the sea.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'westin-koi.jpg'} alt="Orange and white koi in a stream between black rock banks, with small waterfalls at the top and a patch of lawn on one side." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The koi stream, 8:37 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'westin-ocean.jpg'} alt="The rock edge of a pool with green hedges and palms beyond it, a white shade sail, and the blue ocean on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool's ocean edge, 8:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And in the ferns beside a pool there was a <strong>nēnē</strong>, the Hawaiian goose, gray-brown
    with a black head and a striped buff neck.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'nene.jpg'} alt="A nēnē, a gray-brown goose with a black head and a buff, striped neck, standing among ferns on red-brown soil beside a pool." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A nēnē in the ferns, 9:17 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we went back down to Hanalei for shave ice, and then moved to a second rental for the rest of the
    trip, where friends joined us. It sits high on the bluffs, and from about half past
    three my wife's photos come from its lanai, looking straight out to sea over the treetops.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lanai-ocean.jpg'} alt="View from a covered lanai over a dark wooden railing and palms to a wide blue ocean under white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from the lanai, 3:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Hanalei Valley</h2>
  <p>
    On Friday morning, around Princeville, two trees stood out. One was an
    <strong>African tulip tree</strong>, covered in clusters of red-orange flowers against a hard blue sky, with green
    mountains in the distance beyond the lawns. The other is harder to describe: a huge, shaggy dome of long
    striped leaves on top of a thicket of stilt roots, towering over the pickup trucks parked behind it. I think it is a
    hala, the pandanus tree, but I am not sure.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulip-tree.jpg'} alt="A tree with clusters of red-orange flowers on a lawn under a deep blue sky, with green mountains in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An African tulip tree, 10:48 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hala-tree.jpg'} alt="A huge tree with a dome of long yellow-green striped leaves standing on a mass of stilt roots, on a lawn with parked trucks behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A very large, very shaggy tree, 10:53 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Just outside Princeville, the highway passes the <strong>Hanalei Valley Lookout</strong>, and that is where we spent
    the middle of the day. From the rim, the Hanalei River winds through the flat floor of the valley, which is laid out
    in taro fields, out to the curve of Hanalei Bay, with steep green mountains standing behind. My wife's photo from 11:32
    has the whole thing in it, from the river at the bottom to the bay and the clouds.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hanalei-valley.jpg'} alt="View down into a green valley: a river winding through flat fields and trees toward a blue bay, with steep green mountains and white clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hanalei Valley and the bay, 11:32 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By the time I took my own photo, at 12:05, the clouds had come down over the mountains and the light had gone flat,
    but it shows the width of the valley better, with the river making a long bend through the fields below the fence.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hanalei-valley-wide.jpg'} alt="A wide view over a green chain-link fence into Hanalei Valley: a river bending through flat green fields, the bay beyond, and mountains under gray and white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same valley, half an hour later, 12:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the rim, right next to the lookout, is a small open-air stand, <strong>Hanalei Organic</strong>, that sells açaí
    bowls. Its counter faces the valley, so lunch came with the same view: an açaí bowl topped with banana, figs
    and berries and a second bowl next to it, set on the wooden counter with the green valley falling away beside it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'acai-valley.jpg'} alt="An açaí bowl topped with banana, figs and berries and a second bowl on a wooden counter at the edge of an open-air stand, with red ti leaves and a green valley and mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Açaí on the rim, 12:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we went east to <strong>Kīlauea</strong>, and by 1:28 a small haul of fruit was laid out on a wooden
    table: a big papaya, mottled green and orange, a small green citrus, two yellow fruits, a brown one, and a spiky
    green fruit I still can't name. Later we drove down the east coast to <strong>Kapaʻa</strong> and back.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-fruit.jpg'} alt="Fruit laid out on a wooden table: a large mottled papaya, a small green citrus, a spiky green fruit, two yellow fruits and a brown one." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kīlauea haul, 1:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner that evening was on the table by the window, with the sun going down straight into the sea in front of us.
    Forty-five minutes later the sky over the water had gone dark blue, with a last line of orange under the clouds.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dinner-sunset.jpg'} alt="Bowls of food and trays of sushi on a round glass table in front of a window, with the sun setting over the ocean and a palm trunk outside." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner with a sunset, 6:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dusk-sea.jpg'} alt="Dark clouds over the ocean at dusk with a thin band of orange on the horizon, framed by a palm silhouette and a railing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dusk over the sea, 7:32 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Day 4: Turtles, a canyon and the red dirt</h2>
  <p>
    Saturday took us to the south and west sides of the island. It started with one more stop
    in Hanalei, at 10:19, for the second purple thing of the trip: three fried dough balls on a stick, with stripes of
    purple glaze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hanalei-dough.jpg'} alt="Three fried dough balls on a wooden skewer, drizzled with purple glaze, in a white paper tray." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Purple-glazed dough balls, Hanalei, 10:19 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we drove all the way around to the south shore, to <strong>Poʻipū</strong>. Near the beach there, at 2:48,
    green sea turtles were hauled out on the sand, four of them in one frame, dark domed shells against the pale sand,
    with a low reef of black lava and white surf behind them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'poipu-turtles.jpg'} alt="Four green sea turtles resting on a sandy beach, with black lava rocks and breaking white surf behind under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Green sea turtles near Poʻipū, 2:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Waimea Canyon at sunset</h3>
  <p>
    After an early dinner on the south shore we drove west and then up <strong>Waimea Canyon Drive</strong>, in the
    last hour of sun. From the overlook we stopped at,
    at 6:31, the canyon opened out below us: layered walls of red rock and green slopes, the river a thin bright thread
    at the bottom, and the far rim lit gold by the low sun.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-river.jpg'} alt="A deep canyon with green and red slopes and a thin river winding along the bottom, under a pale blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The river at the bottom, 6:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-walls.jpg'} alt="Red-banded canyon walls catching the evening light behind darker green slopes, with the river glinting far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The far rim catching the light, 6:41 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We both photographed the same canyon for ten minutes. In mine from 6:35, a band of sun lies across the plateau on the far
    side, and the slope in front drops through red dirt and shrubs into the shadow. Looking the other way, west from the
    ridge, the grass was lit gold and the sun was going down over the ocean.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-gold.jpg'} alt="A canyon slope dropping away below red dirt, with a band of golden sunlight across the plateau beyond and clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gold on the far side, 6:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-ridge-sunset.jpg'} alt="The sun setting over a grassy ridge and a strip of ocean, behind tall grass and a tree in silhouette." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sun going down over the sea, 6:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A little further up the road, at 6:45, we stopped at the <strong>Red Dirt Falls</strong>. Here the ground is bare,
    rust-red earth, worn into smooth humps and gullies, and a small stream drops over a ledge of it in a narrow white
    waterfall before running off through the grass. Both of us photographed it within a minute of each other.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'red-dirt-falls.jpg'} alt="A narrow white waterfall dropping between smooth humps of rust-red earth into a small pool, with green shrubs and a blue evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The falls, 6:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'red-dirt-falls-his.jpg'} alt="Smooth red earth and pale rock sloping down to a thin waterfall, with trees in evening light along the top of the bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same falls, from further back, 6:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We stayed until the sun was gone. At 6:49 it was setting behind a bank of gray clouds over the red ground, and by
    7:07 the light had gone soft and blue: ridges of red dirt in the foreground, a strip of green, and the ocean on the
    horizon under a pale evening sky. That is the last photo of the trip; we flew home the next day.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'red-dirt-sunset.jpg'} alt="The sun setting in a gap under heavy gray clouds, above trees and a slope of red earth in shadow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the red dirt, 6:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'red-dirt-dusk.jpg'} alt="Ridges of red earth in the dusk with a small stream, a band of green land and the ocean on the horizon under a pale sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last light, 7:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Two sides of one island</h2>
  <p>
    Going back through the photos, the trip splits neatly in two. Three days on the North Shore are all green and blue:
    lawns, ferns, taro fields, a valley under clouds, and the sea from the bluffs. Then one afternoon on the south and
    west sides turns everything red and gold: turtles on a dry beach, a canyon in side light, and bare red earth at
    sunset. It is all the same small island.
  </p>
  <p>
    Five months later we were back in Hawaiʻi, this time on the Big Island and Oʻahu; that trip has its own post,
    <a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">From the Volcano to the Beach</a>.
  </p>
